{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And he considered her a harlot</b> because she was sitting at the crossroads. \"For she had covered her face,\" and he could not see her. And the midrash of our Sages: \"For she had covered her face\" — when she was in his house she was modest, therefore he did not suspect her; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 38:15). And the plain sense as the Rav (Maimonides) understands it is also plausible, for it is the way of a harlot to sit at the entrance to eyes, as it is written (Proverbs 9:14–15), \"And she sits at the door of her house, on a seat by the high places of the city, to call to those who pass by the way,\" and so forth. And it says, \"For she had covered her face,\" and he did not recognize her. But the midrash of our Sages, that she covered her face in her father-in-law's house, is to say that she conducted herself modestly before him in his house and he had never seen her face — how then would he recognize her? And what seems correct in my eyes further, according to the plain sense, is that it says he considered her a harlot because she covered her face, for immediately afterward it says, \"For he did not know that she was his daughter-in-law,\" and the reason is that it is the way of a harlot who sits at the crossroads to have her face partly veiled, covering some of the hair and some of the face, adorning her eyes and lips and revealing the throat and neck. For in order to adorn her face and to speak to him and to take hold of him and kiss him, she would cover part of her face. Moreover, the cult prostitutes who sit by the road, because they also prostitute themselves with men who are relatives, would cover their faces, and so the cult prostitutes do even today in their lands, and when they return to the city they are not recognized. And we learned (Kelim 24:16): There are three kinds of hairnets — that of a young girl is impure by pressing, and that of an old woman is impure with corpse impurity, and that of one who goes out is pure from all of them. \"One who goes out\" is the harlot, \"נָפְקַת בָּרָא\" (one who goes out in the marketplace), who places a hairnet on part of the head — not that she lies upon it, so that it would be impure by pressing, nor to cover her whole head with it, which would render it impure with corpse impurity, but to adorn her locks, so that from beneath the hairnet they are partly visible and partly not — and therefore it is pure from all of them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "The Rav's reading is not found in Maimonides' extant commentary on this verse; Ramban appears to attribute to him the plain-sense reading that a harlot's place is at the crossroads, perhaps by inference or from a lost source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:52.040799482Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}